@page "/docs/workflows"
@layout DocsLayout
@using MudBlazor

<PageTitle>Workflows — AgentBlazor Docs</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-article">
    <section class="docs-hero">
        <p class="docs-hero__eyebrow">Workflow Authoring</p>
        <h1 class="docs-hero__title">Workflows are the primary authoring surface.</h1>
        <p class="docs-hero__lede">
            AgentBlazor is workflow-first. The typical host registers one workflow capability class with
            <code>AddWorkflow&lt;TCapability&gt;()</code>, scopes it to one or more routes, and lets semantic actions drive the app with approvals and deterministic UI execution.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Register a workflow</h2>
                <p>The standard pattern is one route-scoped agent backed by one capability class.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>options.ConfigureBuilder(agentBuilder =&gt;
{
    agentBuilder.AddWorkflow&lt;OpsReviewCapabilities&gt;("ops", agent =&gt;
    {
        agent.WithDescription("Guide the operator through the ops review workflow and explain the approval step.");
        agent.WithRoutePrefixes("/ops-review");
    });
});</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Capability class shape</h2>
                <p>Use semantic actions instead of exposing low-level UI clicks as your primary integration model.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>[AgentCapability("ops_review", Name = "Ops Review", Description = "Starter workflow for a route-scoped review flow.")]
public sealed class OpsReviewCapabilities(OpsReviewService reviewService)
{
    [AgentAction("Assess the current review workflow")]
    public Task&lt;CapabilityResult&gt; AssessAsync()
        =&gt; reviewService.AssessAsync();

    [AgentAction("Prepare the review draft", RequiresApproval = true)]
    public Task&lt;CapabilityResult&gt; PrepareDraftAsync()
        =&gt; reviewService.PrepareDraftAsync();
}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>What matters most</h2>
                <p>Keep the capability model semantic, narrow, and route-aware.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-card-grid">
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Route scoping</strong>
                <p>Use <code>WithRoutePrefixes(...)</code> so the agent is active only where the workflow makes sense.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Approval boundaries</strong>
                <p>Set <code>RequiresApproval = true</code> on actions that mutate real state, submit work, or cross a governance boundary.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Structured results</strong>
                <p>Return <code>CapabilityResult</code> so the runtime can surface success, warnings, blocked states, and next actions consistently.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Host-owned services</strong>
                <p>Inject your own application services and keep business logic there. Capabilities should orchestrate, not own the domain.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Return patterns</h2>
                <p>These are the main response shapes the runtime expects.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>return CapabilityResult.Success("Operation completed.");
return CapabilityResult.Success("Completed.").WithWarning("One item was skipped.");
return CapabilityResult.Success("Draft prepared.").WithNextActions("Review the draft", "Approve the submission");
return CapabilityResult.Blocked("Select a record first.");
return CapabilityResult.Failure("The operation could not be completed.");</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Authoring checklist</h2>
                <p>A small number of rules keeps workflows readable and production-friendly.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-check-grid">
            <div class="docs-check">
                <strong>Do</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>Keep actions semantic and user-facing.</li>
                    <li>Use route prefixes to scope the workflow to the right pages.</li>
                    <li>Mark mutating or risky actions with approval requirements.</li>
                    <li>Return structured results with next actions when possible.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-check">
                <strong>Do not</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>Treat every low-level UI click as the main authoring model.</li>
                    <li>Hide domain logic in capability classes.</li>
                    <li>Leave routes unscoped if the workflow is page-specific.</li>
                    <li>Use chat as a substitute for the normal UI when the normal UI should remain primary.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Read next</h2>
                <p>Move from workflow authoring into chat surfaces or component-level control.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-next-grid">
            <a href="/docs/components" class="docs-next-card">
                <strong>Components</strong>
                <p>See when to stay at the workflow level and when to add deterministic wrappers.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/demo/workflows/response-orchestration?reset=true" class="docs-next-card">
                <strong>Featured Workflow Demo</strong>
                <p>See the workflow-first story on the primary live route.</p>
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>
